\section{SafeLIBERO Benchmark Details}
\label{app:safelibero}

\subsection{Benchmark Structure}

SafeLIBERO \citep{hu2025aegis} extends the standard LIBERO benchmark by introducing collision-relevant obstacles into tabletop manipulation scenes. The benchmark comprises four suites, each containing 4 tasks (SafeLIBERO-Goal defines 5, with 4 evaluated per level):

\paragraph{SafeLIBERO-Spatial} (4 tasks). Obstacles are placed at various locations in the workspace, testing the robot's ability to navigate around static obstructions while performing spatial rearrangement tasks.

\paragraph{SafeLIBERO-Object} (4 tasks). Obstacles are placed on or near the target objects, testing the robot's ability to interact with objects in cluttered proximity.

\paragraph{SafeLIBERO-Goal} (5 tasks, 4 evaluated per level). Obstacles are placed at or near the goal locations, testing the robot's ability to place objects in constrained target regions.

\paragraph{SafeLIBERO-Long} (4 tasks). Multi-step manipulation tasks with obstacles present throughout the workspace, testing collision avoidance over extended horizons.

\subsection{Evaluation Protocol}

\paragraph{Scale.} The benchmark defines 17 unique tasks across the four suites ($4 + 4 + 5 + 4$), of which 16 are evaluated per difficulty level (SafeLIBERO-Goal uses a different subset of 4 out of 5 tasks at each level), yielding $16 \times 2 = 32$ evaluation scenarios.
